============================================================================================
5. TABLE 3 -- FINANCING-COST SENSITIVITY
============================================================================================

A word on what "financing cost" means here.  The 90/60 fund does not borrow at
a quoted rate; it holds futures, whose price already embeds the local money
market rate.  So the financing cost is not a free parameter -- it is whatever
short rates are.  What IS a free parameter is the SPREAD the fund pays over
that rate through the futures basis and the roll.  Both are shown.

5a. HISTORICAL -- an extra financing spread on the 0.60 notional,
    applied to the actual 1990-2026 path.

      spread  90/60 CAGR     vol  Sharpe   vs 100% equities
       0.00%       9.27%  13.01%    0.55             +0.78 pp
       0.25%       9.11%  13.01%    0.53             +0.62 pp
       0.50%       8.95%  13.01%    0.52             +0.46 pp
       1.00%       8.63%  13.02%    0.50             +0.14 pp
       2.00%       7.99%  13.02%    0.45             -0.50 pp

    100% developed equities over the same window: 8.49% CAGR, 14.67% vol, Sharpe 0.45
    A 1pp financing spread costs 0.60pp of return -- it is levied on
    the full 0.60 notional, not on the 0.50 of net leverage.

5b. FORWARD-LOOKING -- absolute financing cost 2% to 6%.

    Anchor from today's curves (2026-09-07):
      weighted ladder yield           4.32%   <- the forward bond return estimate
      weighted short rate             3.44%   <- today's actual financing cost
      implied term premium            0.88%

    Assumptions: equity total return 7.5%/yr (ESTIMATE), bond return = ladder yield 4.32% (held fixed),
    costs 0.27%/yr (documented).

     financing cost  90/60 CAGR  vs All World
               2.0%       8.07%        +0.57 pp
               3.0%       7.57%        +0.07 pp
               4.0%       7.07%        -0.43 pp
               5.0%       6.57%        -0.93 pp
               6.0%       6.07%        -1.43 pp

    d(return)/d(financing) = 0.10 - 0.60 = -0.50: every extra 1pp of
    financing cost costs 0.50pp, because the fund is 50% net levered.
    The level of rates only matters through the SPREAD between the
    ladder yield and the short rate; a parallel rise in both is neutral.

